WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.559
In this lesson, we'll dig into error detection in agentic pipelines.

2
00:00:04.559 --> 00:00:07.039
Silent failures are the hardest to catch.

3
00:00:07.039 --> 00:00:09.199
A step looks like it succeeded,

4
00:00:09.199 --> 00:00:13.720
but returned incorrect or corrupted output with no exception raised.

5
00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:17.040
We'll cover output validation, state verification,

6
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:20.120
sanity checks, validation gates, and logging.

7
00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.680
Let's get into it.

8
00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:27.280
The worst failure in an agentic pipeline is the one that never announces itself.

9
00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:32.319
A silent failure is a tool or agent returning output that appears successful,

10
00:00:32.319 --> 00:00:36.400
but is incorrect or corrupted with no error signal raised.

11
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.479
So downstream stages proceed on bad data without any warning.

12
00:00:40.479 --> 00:00:44.319
A validation gate is a checkpoint that inspects step output

13
00:00:44.319 --> 00:00:48.720
against defined criteria before allowing execution to continue,

14
00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.840
and it catches both explicit errors and silent ones.

15
00:00:52.799 --> 00:00:57.439
Error propagation is the mechanism by which a failure in one stage

16
00:00:57.439 --> 00:00:59.520
flows into downstream stages,

17
00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:03.040
and causes cascading failures across the pipeline.

18
00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:06.800
Those patterns are the foundation for building systems that fail loudly,

19
00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.959
rather than silently.

20
00:01:08.959 --> 00:01:13.519
A silent failure is dangerous precisely because it produces no signal.

21
00:01:13.519 --> 00:01:16.720
No exception is thrown. No error code is returned.

22
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:18.879
The pipeline continues normally.

23
00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:24.000
Downstream stages receive the bad output and build further work on top of it.

24
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.120
By the time the error surfaces as a visible wrong result,

25
00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.239
its origin may be several steps back in the pipeline.

26
00:01:30.239 --> 00:01:32.639
Tracing it becomes a forensics exercise.

27
00:01:32.639 --> 00:01:36.639
Error code-based retry logic doesn't help here at all,

28
00:01:36.639 --> 00:01:39.120
because there's no error code to catch.

29
00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:44.879
The only way to detect silent failures is to actively inspect output at each stage boundary,

30
00:01:44.879 --> 00:01:49.680
rather than relying on the tool or API to tell you when something went wrong.

31
00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:54.239
Validation gates are the mechanism for doing that inspection reliably.

32
00:01:54.239 --> 00:01:57.680
The first detection strategy is output validation.

33
00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:00.879
Before passing a step's result to the next stage,

34
00:02:00.879 --> 00:02:03.760
inspect it against defined criteria.

35
00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:06.800
Schema checks verify required fields are present,

36
00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:11.360
types are correct, and there are no unexpected null values.

37
00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:15.759
Range checks confirm numeric values fall within expected bounds,

38
00:02:15.759 --> 00:02:18.240
and strings are within length limits.

39
00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:22.240
Completeness checks verify all required sections are populated,

40
00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:24.720
and responses weren't truncated.

41
00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.320
Together, these three check types catch the structural errors

42
00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:33.839
and silent failures that status code only handling misses entirely.

43
00:02:33.839 --> 00:02:37.440
Output validation is the first gate between steps in a pipeline,

44
00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.479
and it handles the majority of silent failure cases

45
00:02:40.479 --> 00:02:43.279
you're likely to encounter in production agentic systems.

46
00:02:44.479 --> 00:02:49.279
Output validation breaks into two distinct layers that complement each other.

47
00:02:49.279 --> 00:02:53.839
Schema validation checks structure, required fields, correct types,

48
00:02:53.839 --> 00:02:57.440
no unexpected nulls, it's fast, automatable,

49
00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:01.199
and catches formatting errors and truncated responses,

50
00:03:01.199 --> 00:03:05.360
but it doesn't verify whether the content is actually correct or meaningful.

51
00:03:05.919 --> 00:03:10.320
Semantic validation goes further and asks whether the output is plausible,

52
00:03:10.399 --> 00:03:11.839
given the input.

53
00:03:11.839 --> 00:03:14.080
Are values in expected ranges?

54
00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:17.199
Does the response make sense in context?

55
00:03:17.199 --> 00:03:19.199
Does it address what was asked?

56
00:03:19.199 --> 00:03:22.320
Semantic validation is harder to automate fully,

57
00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.960
but it's the layer that catches silent failures that pass schema checks.

58
00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:30.000
A complete detection strategy needs both layers

59
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.240
working together at each stage boundary.

60
00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:37.119
State verification is the second detection strategy.

61
00:03:37.199 --> 00:03:38.880
After a step completes,

62
00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:42.559
check whether the world actually matches what the agent expects.

63
00:03:43.279 --> 00:03:44.960
After a write operation,

64
00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:48.160
confirm the record was persisted correctly by reading it back.

65
00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:50.479
After a transformation,

66
00:03:50.479 --> 00:03:53.600
verify the output reflects the intended change,

67
00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:56.320
rather than just trusting the return value of the call.

68
00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:00.559
After an API call that's supposed to trigger a side effect,

69
00:04:00.559 --> 00:04:02.960
validate that the side effect actually occurred.

70
00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:06.559
State verification catches a common failure pattern,

71
00:04:06.559 --> 00:04:08.960
where an action appeared to succeed.

72
00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:11.119
The call returned without an error,

73
00:04:11.119 --> 00:04:14.399
but nothing actually changed in the underlying system.

74
00:04:14.399 --> 00:04:16.000
Without state verification,

75
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:19.679
those cases are silent failures that proceed undetected.

76
00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.920
The third detection strategy is the sanity check.

77
00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:28.000
After output validation confirms the structure is correct,

78
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:31.760
sanity checks ask whether the output is actually plausible,

79
00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:33.519
given what was input.

80
00:04:33.519 --> 00:04:35.839
Is the output length reasonable?

81
00:04:35.839 --> 00:04:38.799
A one-word answer to a complex multipart query

82
00:04:38.799 --> 00:04:41.119
is a signal that something went wrong,

83
00:04:41.119 --> 00:04:42.559
even if the schema is valid.

84
00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:46.880
Are the key entities from the input reflected in the output?

85
00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:49.440
If a query mentioned three specific items

86
00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:52.959
and none of them appear in the response, that's suspicious.

87
00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:55.040
Does the output contradict facts

88
00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:57.040
that were provided in the input context?

89
00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:00.160
Sanity checks catch the semantic errors

90
00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.239
that pass structural validation,

91
00:05:02.239 --> 00:05:05.920
but would still silently corrupt downstream processing

92
00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:07.119
if left undetected.

93
00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:10.640
Validation gates are the structural mechanism

94
00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:13.119
that implements those detection strategies

95
00:05:13.119 --> 00:05:15.440
at stage boundaries in a pipeline.

96
00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:17.519
A gate has four responsibilities.

97
00:05:17.519 --> 00:05:20.559
First, it inspects the output using schema,

98
00:05:20.559 --> 00:05:23.040
range, completeness, and plausibility checks.

99
00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:25.519
Second, if validation fails,

100
00:05:25.519 --> 00:05:28.239
it can trigger a retry with corrected input

101
00:05:28.239 --> 00:05:29.440
or a modified prompt.

102
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.079
Third, if retries are exhausted,

103
00:05:32.079 --> 00:05:35.440
it routes to a fallback path or degrades gracefully.

104
00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:38.239
Fourth, if fallback also fails,

105
00:05:38.239 --> 00:05:40.239
it escalates to a human reviewer

106
00:05:40.239 --> 00:05:42.959
or sends an alert to a monitoring system.

107
00:05:42.959 --> 00:05:45.040
The exam trap worth knowing

108
00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:47.920
is that gates checking only for error codes

109
00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:49.200
miss silent failures.

110
00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:52.399
Gates need to validate semantic correctness,

111
00:05:52.399 --> 00:05:53.519
not just status codes.

112
00:05:54.799 --> 00:05:56.320
Logging and observability

113
00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:58.799
are what makes silent failures diagnosable

114
00:05:58.799 --> 00:05:59.600
after they occur.

115
00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:03.279
Log inputs and outputs at every stage boundary,

116
00:06:03.279 --> 00:06:05.359
not just when errors are raised.

117
00:06:05.359 --> 00:06:07.119
If you only log on error,

118
00:06:07.119 --> 00:06:09.760
you have no record of what the stage received

119
00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:11.679
when a silent failure happened.

120
00:06:11.679 --> 00:06:13.519
Use structured log formats

121
00:06:13.519 --> 00:06:17.279
so individual fields are queryable and filterable.

122
00:06:17.279 --> 00:06:20.640
Execution traces record the full sequence of steps,

123
00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:22.320
tool calls, and their outcomes.

124
00:06:22.959 --> 00:06:25.119
That trace is what lets you reconstruct

125
00:06:25.119 --> 00:06:27.600
what happened when a user reports a wrong result.

126
00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:29.760
Anomaly detection on logs

127
00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:32.239
can flag unusual patterns proactively

128
00:06:32.239 --> 00:06:33.440
before users notice.

129
00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:37.519
Observability is what transforms a silent failure

130
00:06:37.519 --> 00:06:39.119
from an invisible event

131
00:06:39.119 --> 00:06:41.440
into a diagnosable and fixable one.

132
00:06:43.119 --> 00:06:45.519
Detection is the work of making a pipeline

133
00:06:45.519 --> 00:06:46.640
tell you when it's wrong.

134
00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:48.720
Nothing raises an exception

135
00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:51.200
when a step returns confident garbage.

136
00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:53.920
So the signal has to be something

137
00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:56.640
you built on purpose at every stage boundary.

138
00:06:57.279 --> 00:06:59.279
That's what keeps one bad output

139
00:06:59.279 --> 00:07:01.519
from quietly poisoning everything after it.

140
00:07:02.079 --> 00:07:03.839
Just remember that a check

141
00:07:03.839 --> 00:07:06.000
only covers the dimensions you wrote it for,

142
00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:08.000
so one is usually not enough.

143
00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:10.160
That does it for this lesson.

144
00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:11.600
I'll see you in the next one.

